**Steps to reproduce or a small repository showing the problem:**

Given the following entities:

    @Entity()
    export class Match {

      @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
      id: number;

      @Column(type => Team, { prefix: 'red' })
      red: Team;

      @Column(type => Team, { prefix: 'blue' })
      blue: Team;

    }

    export class Team {

      @JoinColumn()
      @ManyToOne(type => Player)
      striker: Player;

      @JoinColumn()
      @ManyToOne(type => Player)
      defender: Player;

    }

    @Entity()
    export class Player {

      @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
      id: number;

      @Column()
      name: string;

    }

TypeORM does not take in account the `prefix` option, i.e., it generates the following schema for the entity `match`:

    id | strikerId | defenderId

instead of what I think it should be:

    id | redStrikerId | redDefenderId | blueStrikerId | blueDefenderId

This is pretty annoying because it prevent the user to properly reduce code duplication.
The issue apparently occurs only with the `@JoinColumn`s of the embedded entity. The default naming strategy does not apply either. It could be related to #3132 and #928.
